
This document lists everything (languages, tools, frameworks, and services) used to build, run, and deploy this project, mapped to each step of the assignment.

## 1. Languages used

| Language | Where it's used | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| JavaScript (Node.js runtime) | `server.js` | Back-end logic, API routes, data handling |
| JavaScript (browser runtime) | `public/index.html` (`<script>`) | Front-end logic, calling the API, updating the page |
| HTML | `public/index.html` | Page structure / UI elements |
| CSS | `public/index.html` (`<style>`) | Styling and layout |
| JSON | Used as the data format everywhere | Request bodies, API responses, `package.json` |

## 2. Runtime and package manager

| Tool | Version used | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| **Node.js** | v18+ recommended | JavaScript runtime that runs `server.js` outside the browser |
| **npm** (Node Package Manager) | bundled with Node.js | Installs and manages dependencies listed in `package.json` |

## 3. Back-end dependencies (installed via `npm install`)

| Package | Version | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| **express** | ^4.19.2 | Web framework used to create the server, define REST routes, and serve static files |
| **cors** | ^2.8.5 | Middleware that allows the front-end to make requests to the API without being blocked by browser security restrictions |

These are declared in `package.json` and locked to exact versions in `package-lock.json`.

## 4. Front-end technologies (no extra installs required)

| Technology | Purpose |
|---|---|
| **Fetch API** (`fetch()`) | Built into all modern browsers; used to send GET/POST/PUT/DELETE requests to the API |
| **DOM APIs** (`document.createElement`, `addEventListener`, etc.) | Used to dynamically build and update the task list on the page without a refresh |
| Vanilla JavaScript | No front-end framework (React, Vue, etc.) was used — plain JS was enough for this project's scope |

## 5. Development and deployment tools

| Tool | Purpose |
|---|---|
| **Git** | Version control; tracks changes to the project |
| **GitHub** | Hosts the project's source code online; connected repo triggers deployments |
| **Render** | Cloud hosting platform; runs `npm install` then `npm start` to serve the app live at a public URL |

## 6. How each requirement/tool maps to the assignment steps

**Step 1 — Create RESTful API endpoints for CRUD operations**
- Built with: **Express** (`server.js`)
- `GET /api/tasks`, `GET /api/tasks/:id`, `POST /api/tasks`, `PUT /api/tasks/:id`, `DELETE /api/tasks/:id`

**Step 2 — Develop a front-end interface that interacts with your own server API**
- Built with: **HTML, CSS, vanilla JavaScript**, served as static files by Express (`express.static`)

**Step 3 — Fetch and display data from your API on the front end**
- Built with: the **Fetch API** (`fetch()`) and **DOM manipulation** in `public/index.html`, calling the endpoints defined in Step 1

## 7. Data storage

| Approach | Notes |
|---|---|
| In-memory JavaScript array (`let tasks = [...]` in `server.js`) | No external database was used. Data resets whenever the server restarts. This keeps the project focused on the API/front-end integration itself rather than database setup. |

